# Cardinality of Continuous Functions ℝ → ℝ

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only the question, no solution, sketch, or answer. Proceeding.

## Claim

The set $\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})$ of all continuous functions $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ has cardinality $\mathfrak{c} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, the cardinality of the continuum.

## Proof

We establish the cardinality by two inequalities.

### Upper bound: $|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})| \le \mathfrak{c}$

Let $\mathbb{Q}\subset\mathbb{R}$ be the rationals. Since $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$ and every $f\in\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})$ is continuous, $f$ is completely determined by its restriction $f|_{\mathbb{Q}}:\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{R}$.

Indeed, for every $x\in\mathbb{R}$, pick any sequence $(q_n)\subset\mathbb{Q}$ with $q_n\to x$; then $f(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}f(q_n)$, so the value $f(x)$ is forced by $f|_{\mathbb{Q}}$. Hence the restriction map
$$\Phi:\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})\to\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{Q}},\qquad \Phi(f)=f|_{\mathbb{Q}}$$
is injective. Therefore
$$|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})|\le|\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{Q}}|=|\mathbb{R}|^{|\mathbb{Q}|}=\mathfrak{c}^{\aleph_0}=\left(2^{\aleph_0}\right)^{\aleph_0}=2^{\aleph_0\cdot\aleph_0}=2^{\aleph_0}=\mathfrak{c}.$$

### Lower bound: $|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})|\ge\mathfrak{c}$

For each $a\in\mathbb{R}$, the constant function $f_a(x)=a$ is continuous. The map $a\mapsto f_a$ is an injection $\mathbb{R}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})$, so
$$|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})|\ge|\mathbb{R}|=\mathfrak{c}.$$

### Conclusion

Combining both inequalities,
$$|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})|=\mathfrak{c}=2^{\aleph_0}.$$

